  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/293e79685a3f4da205d986bcf4331e4b4f43c0a48fe183d27f93aaee6bb17b99.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/cc6e04660f00d04307715159c25aa2860ca08af74ed36fc9b0be15d10ee94a27.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/744aeb24bb4fd4487b35ffd789d957879cb0c3ee92e40519f71b65ee5ee76dcf.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,2)=6 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,4)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 6+4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=80.\n$$",
